Options of the tar Utility
One of the following tar command actions is required to perform a tar operation:
-c or --create : Create an archive file.
-t or --list : List the contents of an archive.
-x or --extract : Extract an archive.

The following tar command general options are often included:
-v or --verbose : Show the files that are being archived or extracted during the tar operation.
-f or --file : Follow this option with the archive file name to create or open.
-p or --preserve-permissions : Preserve the original file permissions when extracting.
--xattrs : Enable extended attribute support, and store extended file attributes.
--selinux : Enable SELinux context support, and store SELinux file contexts.

The following tar command compression options are used to select an algorithm:
-a or --auto-compress : Use the archive's suffix to determine the algorithm to use.
-z or --gzip : Use the gzip compression algorithm, which results in a .tar.gz suffix.
-j or --bzip2 : Use the bzip2 compression algorithm, which results in a .tar.bz2 suffix.
-J or --xz : Use the xz compression algorithm, which results in a .tar.xz suffix.

-----------------------example------------------------------
tar -cf mybackup.tar myapp1.log myapp2.log myapp3.log = creates the mybackup.tar archive to contain the myapp1.log, myapp2.log, and myapp2.log files
ls mybackup.tar 
+ A user must have read permissions on the target files that are being archived. For example, creating an archive in the /etc directory requires root privileges, because only privileged users can read all /etc files. 
tar -cf /root/etc-backup.tar /etc = root user creates the /root/etc-backup.tar archive of the /etc directory.
tar -tf /root/etc.tar = t - list achieve content that are specified with the f option
tar -xpf /home/user/myscripts.tar = 
tar -czf /root/etcbackup.tar.gz /etc
tar -cjf /root/logbackup.tar.bz2 /var/log
tar -cJf /root/sshconfig.tar.xz /etc/ssh
tar -tf /root/etcbackup.tar.gz
tar -xzf /root/etcbackup.tar.xz
tar -tf /root/logbackup.tar
gzip -l file.tar.gz
-------------------Transfer Files Between Systems Securely--------------------
Secure File Transfer Program (SFTP)
The put command uploads a file to the remote system. 
The get command downloads a file from the remote system. 
The exit command exits the sftp session.
work the same way with ls, cd, mkdir, rmdir, and pwd commands with remote file system
tambah 'l' dekat dekat depan kalau nak access local host. exp: lcd, lpwd

sftp remoteuser@remotehost
sftp> help - list available commands in sftp
sftp> pwd - print current dir on remote host
sftp> lpwd - print current dir on local host
sftp> mkdir hostbackup - create dir
sftp > cd hostbackup - go to the new dir
sftp > put /etc/hosts - upload the dir
sftp> put -r directory - to upload whole directory recursively
sftp> get /etc/yum.conf - to download file
sftp remoteuser@remotehost:/home/remoteuser/remotefile 
sftp: Starts the secure file transfer program.
remoteuser@remotehost: Specifies your username and the address of the remote computer.
:: Separation character indicating that a specific file path follows.
/home/remoteuser/remotefile: The exact folder path and name of the file you want to download.










